1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,320
Hey everyone! We are back and we are ...

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:06,840
going to have a look at how and what we produce relates ...

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,560
to the costs we incur. Let's jump into it.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,906
Let's first start by recalling how much content you ...

5
00:00:13,906 --> 00:00:17,122
created when you added workers to your content ...

6
00:00:17,122 --> 00:00:19,431
creation process. With no labor,

7
00:00:19,431 --> 00:00:20,998
nothing was produced.

8
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:25,808
When you added one person, your total content increased ...

9
00:00:25,808 --> 00:00:28,519
to 2 and your marginal product was 2.

10
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,909
Adding a second person increased your total product ...

11
00:00:32,909 --> 00:00:35,039
to 5 and marginal product by 3.

12
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,573
Adding a third person increased your total product ...

13
00:00:39,573 --> 00:00:41,789
to 9 and marginal product by 4.

14
00:00:41,789 --> 00:00:45,158
At this point, your marginal product peaked.

15
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,440
Adding a fourth person increased your total product ...

16
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,520
to 12 and marginal product by 3.

17
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:54,560
Here, diminishing marginal returns sets in.

18
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,147
We can only get so much out of the fixed inputs we have.

19
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:02,533
To change this, we'd have to add more capital ...

20
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:05,278
to our content production process.

21
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,594
Adding a fifth person increased your total product ...

22
00:01:09,594 --> 00:01:11,719
to 14 and marginal product by 2.

23
00:01:11,719 --> 00:01:15,233
Adding a sixth person increased your total product ...

24
00:01:15,233 --> 00:01:17,358
to 15 and marginal product by 1.

25
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,490
Adding a seventh person did not change your total product ...

26
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,705
and your marginal product is 0.

27
00:01:24,705 --> 00:01:27,432
It's not a good idea to add this worker,

28
00:01:27,432 --> 00:01:31,437
as they create no additional content for your business.

29
00:01:31,437 --> 00:01:33,311
Next up, let's talk costs.

30
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:35,952
First, let's address the fixed costs ...

31
00:01:35,952 --> 00:01:38,849
associated with running your business.

32
00:01:38,849 --> 00:01:42,598
These are costs that do not change as output changes.

33
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:45,154
Remember that computer you bought.

34
00:01:45,154 --> 00:01:47,795
Let's say you took out a loan to buy it.

35
00:01:47,795 --> 00:01:51,289
You can create as much content on it as you want and ...

36
00:01:51,289 --> 00:01:53,760
that loan payment does not change.

37
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,060
Here, we have $1,000 as our fixed ...

38
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:58,105
cost. As we add more labor and ...

39
00:01:58,105 --> 00:02:00,661
produce more, our fixed costs do not ...

40
00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:03,217
change. You'll also notice that you ...

41
00:02:03,217 --> 00:02:06,711
have to pay $1,000 even if you producing nothing.

42
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:11,780
That's because this is a cost you've incurred and it doesn't ...

43
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:13,959
matter if you produce or not.

44
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,256
Next up variable costs. These are costs that do ...

45
00:02:18,256 --> 00:02:20,640
change as production changes.

46
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,949
As you add more workers to your content creation process ...

47
00:02:24,949 --> 00:02:29,442
you incur additional costs. Let's look at the first person ...

48
00:02:29,442 --> 00:02:32,559
you hire. It will cost us $800 to hire this ...

49
00:02:32,559 --> 00:02:37,877
person on a weekly basis and they help increase output or content by 2.

50
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:42,436
The next person we hire adds an additional $700 per week,

51
00:02:42,436 --> 00:02:46,517
and adds 3 additional types of content per week for a total ...

52
00:02:46,517 --> 00:02:48,557
of 5. Let's go ahead and add in ...

53
00:02:48,557 --> 00:02:52,468
the rest of the workers and you'll see that our variable ...

54
00:02:52,468 --> 00:02:55,954
costs continue to grow because we are adding more ...

55
00:02:55,954 --> 00:02:58,505
to the content production process.

56
00:02:58,505 --> 00:03:01,141
We go from $600 with the third worker,

57
00:03:01,141 --> 00:03:04,542
All the way to $1,200 for the last worker we hire.

58
00:03:04,542 --> 00:03:08,878
Notice that our variable cost first decrease then increase.

59
00:03:08,878 --> 00:03:11,684
Why does this happen? Several reasons.

60
00:03:11,684 --> 00:03:14,320
It could be labor market conditions,

61
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,976
or it could be the marginal product that each worker ...

62
00:03:17,976 --> 00:03:20,527
produces is tied to their wage rate.

63
00:03:20,527 --> 00:03:24,098
Take a look at the demand for resources chapter for ...

64
00:03:24,098 --> 00:03:27,669
more explanations. Before we get to our last cost,

65
00:03:27,669 --> 00:03:31,240
there's one other type of cost called a mixed cost.

66
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:34,131
This combines fixed and variable costs.

67
00:03:34,131 --> 00:03:37,192
For example, if you do not use electricity,

68
00:03:37,192 --> 00:03:39,488
you still have to pay a base rate.

69
00:03:39,488 --> 00:03:41,699
The base rate is the fixed cost,

70
00:03:41,699 --> 00:03:44,590
and the usage becomes the variable cost.

71
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:47,907
Okay! We now have our total costs ...

72
00:03:47,907 --> 00:03:51,744
of producing content. Total cost is the summation ...

73
00:03:51,744 --> 00:03:56,130
of fixed and variable costs. Notice that with no workers,

74
00:03:56,130 --> 00:03:59,236
I still have total costs equal to $1,000.

75
00:03:59,236 --> 00:04:02,799
That means I still have expenses even if I don't ...

76
00:04:02,799 --> 00:04:05,540
employ anyone or produce anything.

77
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:09,651
My total costs also follow the same path as my variable ...

78
00:04:09,651 --> 00:04:11,844
costs. That's because of how ...

79
00:04:11,844 --> 00:04:15,316
production and costs are tied to one another.

80
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,727
Here, we have marginal costs,

81
00:04:18,727 --> 00:04:22,681
which are the change in total costs over the change in ...

82
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:25,557
output. Notice that my marginal costs ...

83
00:04:25,557 --> 00:04:29,601
start high and then fall to their lowest point with the ...

84
00:04:29,601 --> 00:04:31,039
third worker. Now,

85
00:04:31,039 --> 00:04:35,173
adding the fourth worker increases my marginal costs.

86
00:04:35,173 --> 00:04:38,139
The fifth, sixth and seventh workers do ...

87
00:04:38,139 --> 00:04:40,655
as well. This is all because of how ...

88
00:04:40,655 --> 00:04:44,969
diminishing returns kicks in to the production process.

89
00:04:44,969 --> 00:04:48,564
Adding more workers to a fixed amount of capital ...

90
00:04:48,564 --> 00:04:50,811
reduces how much is produced.

91
00:04:50,811 --> 00:04:55,125
But also adding more workers increases your cost of doing ...

92
00:04:55,125 --> 00:04:56,473
business. Hence,

93
00:04:56,473 --> 00:04:59,708
as you increase your production and labor,

94
00:04:59,708 --> 00:05:03,033
your output diminishes and your costs rise.

95
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:05,960
Let's look at our cost curves.

96
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,280
We have our fixed, variable and total cost curves displayed.

97
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,409
First, notice the fixed cost ...

98
00:05:13,409 --> 00:05:17,350
curve—the red line—is horizontal at $1,000.

99
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:22,639
This is because our costs do not change as our output changes.

100
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,838
Next, noticed the variable cost ...

101
00:05:25,838 --> 00:05:29,100
curve starts at $0. This is because if we don't ...

102
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:32,190
employ anyone, then we don't have variable ...

103
00:05:32,190 --> 00:05:34,508
costs. The shape of the variable ...

104
00:05:34,508 --> 00:05:38,371
cost curve also picks up the mechanics of diminishing ...

105
00:05:38,371 --> 00:05:41,118
returns. Up to 9 units being produces,

106
00:05:41,118 --> 00:05:44,466
our costs grow slowly. After 9 units produced,

107
00:05:44,466 --> 00:05:48,157
our costs grow with each additional unit produced.

108
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:51,152
Lastly, the total cost curve—the ...

109
00:05:51,152 --> 00:05:55,390
green line—starts at the fixed cost line then follows the ...

110
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:58,127
same path as the variable cost curve.

111
00:05:58,127 --> 00:06:00,599
First, we have to pay a cost even if ...

112
00:06:00,599 --> 00:06:02,806
nothing is produced. Second,

113
00:06:02,806 --> 00:06:07,132
total cost is the combination of fixed and variable costs,

114
00:06:07,132 --> 00:06:12,076
so the distance between total and variable cost is just fixed cost.

115
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:17,221
Let's finish with a look a how production and costs relate.

116
00:06:17,221 --> 00:06:21,083
Here we've overlayed our production function on the ...

117
00:06:21,083 --> 00:06:22,399
cost curve graph.

118
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:25,750
Notice up to 9 units of content created,

119
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:29,524
our costs grow slowly and our production grows fast.

120
00:06:29,524 --> 00:06:32,869
This is because we've only added a few workers,

121
00:06:32,869 --> 00:06:36,471
so our costs are low, but they are making efficient ...

122
00:06:36,471 --> 00:06:39,558
use out of the resources available to them.

123
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:43,192
As we continue to expand, our costs grow more,

124
00:06:43,192 --> 00:06:46,059
but our output grows slowly. Eventually,

125
00:06:46,059 --> 00:06:49,909
we get to the point where production flatlines and costs ...

126
00:06:49,909 --> 00:06:53,759
continue growing. We definitely don't want to be there.

127
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:55,365
Alright! That's it.

128
00:06:55,365 --> 00:06:57,825
Production and costs (not all) are in the books.

129
00:06:57,825 --> 00:07:00,100
We look at the margin/average relationship ...

130
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:01,637
next. Hope you've enjoyed it!
